# SFT execution status v0.112

2026-09-24. The import-inert listener-adapter and independent guardian
candidates are implemented, hardened and locally verified. **This is not an
integrated native write guard; Gate 5 remains incomplete.** No checkpoint,
tokenizer or dataset payload was read. No native filter/listener/ioctl, pidfd,
cgroup, release socket, worker, remote mutation, GPU operation, inference or
training occurred. The seven successor files remain local and unpushed.

## Completed

- The private listener candidate fixes the Linux v6.8 x86_64 notification ABI,
  reads back candidate kernel sizes/action support, sets and reads back
  no-new-privileges, installs only the frozen listener program, requires one
  exact transfer callback acknowledgement, verifies descriptor stability and
  closes its local copy. The final seal is a distinct same-owner one-shot after
  that handshake. Public installation remains unconditionally closed.
- The private supervisor candidate owns one adopted CLOEXEC listener descriptor
  and enforces one receive, one ID-validity check and one exact CONTINUE response
  per outstanding notification. All ambiguous or malformed operations consume
  it terminally without retry. Native SCM_RIGHTS receipt/origin and the target
  ABI are not established by these local tests.
- Notification receive may block, so the design now requires a separately
  killable broker process. It cannot share the guardian's failure domain or rely
  on a cancellable thread.
- The guardian binds the frozen cgroup lease, parent pidfd, an owned duplicate of
  the borrowed broker pidfd and a root-owned CLOEXEC O_RDWR local seqpacket
  release descriptor. The fixed release byte uses per-call nonblocking and
  no-SIGPIPE flags. Pidfd readiness catches exited zombies even if signal zero
  still succeeds.
- After binding, terminal priority is fixed as parent loss, deadline, broker
  loss and then workload empty. Every admitted terminal path retires the owned
  cgroup and makes one generation-stable broker-stop attempt. Reports retain
  only aggregate state and explicitly keep all unproved origin, receipt, reap,
  exclusivity, containment and native-enforcement claims false.

## Verification

**697 unique tests pass on CPython 3.11.15 and 3.13.7**, with no failures,
errors or skips: 37 listener tests, 52 guardian tests and 608 prior regressions.
All native boundaries in the 89 new tests are synthetic.

An independent review found no concrete blocker in the local candidate. It
confirmed the seqpacket send flags, signal sanitation, borrowed-versus-owned
pidfd handling, zero-time readiness checks and cleanup/report semantics. The
review also confirmed that broker origin/receipt/reap, release-channel origin and
exclusivity, release consumption, release/liveness atomicity, guardian-death
containment and native enforcement remain unproved.

Syntax/AST checks pass on both runtimes; maximum line length is 100 and trailing
whitespace is zero. Ruff was unavailable and no replacement was downloaded.
All 61 earlier bound files, 78 qualified native source rows and 39 worker source
rows matched their recorded hashes. Earlier evidence and resource envelopes are
unchanged; tracked and staged diffs are empty.

Five public Linux v6.8 references were read to check the candidate ABI. There
were no project/runtime network operations or remote mutations. The kernel
documentation describes USER_NOTIF listener transfer as an intended supervisor
pattern and warns that receiving a notification can wait; this supports the
separate broker-process boundary but does not qualify the target host:

- <https://www.kernel.org/doc/html/v6.8/userspace-api/seccomp_filter.html>
- <https://raw.githubusercontent.com/torvalds/linux/v6.8/include/uapi/linux/seccomp.h>
- <https://raw.githubusercontent.com/torvalds/linux/v6.8/kernel/seccomp.c>
- <https://raw.githubusercontent.com/torvalds/linux/v6.8/arch/x86/entry/syscalls/syscall_64.tbl>
- <https://raw.githubusercontent.com/torvalds/linux/v6.8/include/uapi/asm-generic/ioctl.h>

